experiment: ctxaxis
  instrument        ctxaxis   trial seed 0000000001355b0c   428 trials per creature
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  ctxaxis -- the oracle chain proved the DERIVED INDEX caused the F1 bit's
  decay and that a perfect index REVERSES it. This asks the next question
  down: is F1 worse than F2 because the EAR does not separate 118 Hz, or
  because the CLUSTERING RULE throws the separation away?
  the feature   `feat_ema`, the ear's rate EMA at reward time -- the exact
                vector DNA v53 source 4 clusters, on kGridWords' 2x2 grid.
  the scoring   one BIT at a time, held out, with any slot-to-bit map fitted
                on the training half only. So chance is exactly 0.50 and the
                F1-minus-F2 difference has a null of exactly ZERO.
  the columns   ear (a supervised READ of the feature, not an absolute
                ceiling -- it fits 2048 parameters where a partition fits 4),
                kern (the kernel's own rule, from the origin), raw (seeded
                from data: isolates INIT), z (per-channel SCALE), sph
                (overall MAGNITUDE).
  the primary   bit F1 minus bit F2, paired per creature, on `ear` and on
                `kern`. An asymmetric ear means RESOLUTION; a symmetric ear
                with an asymmetric kernel means the RULE, and is actionable.
  read-only     no learning, no genome field, nothing shipped.
  18 creatures, 4 slots, 8 inits per seeded column.

  usable rows per creature 417.3 of 428 trials the budget buys (the rest were slept through).

  column   n     bit F1           bit F2           F1 - F2 paired
  ear       18   0.850 +/- 0.020    0.965 +/- 0.006    -0.115 +/- 0.021 (5.4 SE)
  ear-z     18   0.850 +/- 0.020    0.965 +/- 0.006    -0.115 +/- 0.021 (5.4 SE)
  ear-sph   18   0.646 +/- 0.027    0.951 +/- 0.014    -0.305 +/- 0.032 (9.4 SE)
  kern      18   0.827 +/- 0.011    0.781 +/- 0.023    +0.046 +/- 0.031 (1.5 SE)
  raw       18   0.852 +/- 0.011    0.739 +/- 0.015    +0.113 +/- 0.025 (4.5 SE)
  z         18   0.879 +/- 0.014    0.786 +/- 0.010    +0.093 +/- 0.012 (7.7 SE)
  sph       18   0.916 +/- 0.008    0.900 +/- 0.010    +0.016 +/- 0.005 (3.0 SE)

  VOID CHECK, shuffled labels on `ear`: 0.512 +/- 0.008 (F1), 0.501 +/- 0.009 (F2)
                against a constructed chance of 0.500.

  THE PRIMARY, as registered. ear F1-F2 -0.115 +/- 0.021 (5.4 SE);  kern F1-F2 +0.046 +/- 0.031 (1.5 SE)

  THE REGISTERED RESOLUTION BRANCH FIRES: the supervised reader gets the
  F1 bit out of the ear worse than the F2 bit, 0.850 against 0.965, 5.4 SE
  on a quantity whose null is exactly zero.

  AND IT CANNOT BE READ AS A RESOLUTION LIMIT. THE PRE-REGISTRATION WAS
  MIS-SPECIFIED, and the table refutes it from inside.

  WHY: every column is a DECODER scored held-out, so every column is a
  LOWER BOUND on what the feature holds, and two bounds cannot be
  subtracted. The table shows that directly -- `ear-sph` reads bit F1 at
  0.646 where `ear` reads 0.850 on the SAME feature, so that reader lost
  0.204 of content and the apparent asymmetry GREW to 9.4 SE. An asymmetry
  that moves with the reader is a fact about readers.

  WHAT IT CAN SAY: the feature holds bit F1 at AT LEAST 0.916 (sph) and
  bit F2 at AT LEAST 0.965 (ear). Those bounds differ by 0.049, where the
  reader-based "asymmetries" above read 0.115 and 0.305. The resolution
  question is therefore NOT SETTLED by this run, and must not be cited as
  settled -- 118 Hz being harder than 300 Hz remains plausible and
  unmeasured. Settling it needs a CEILING, not another decoder.

  WHAT THE PARTITION LOSES against the best supervised read of the same
  feature (ear), per bit:
    bit F1   reader 0.850, kernel 0.827, best partition 0.916  (gap +0.066)
    bit F2   reader 0.965, kernel 0.781, best partition 0.900  (gap -0.064)

  AND WHICH COMPONENT, one variable at a time (bit F1 then bit F2):
    bit F1  kern 0.827 -> raw 0.852 (INIT)  -> z 0.879 (per-channel SCALE)  -> sph 0.916 (overall MAGNITUDE)
    bit F2  kern 0.781 -> raw 0.739 (INIT)  -> z 0.786 (per-channel SCALE)  -> sph 0.900 (overall MAGNITUDE)
    the supervised reads of the same feature: raw 0.850/0.965, z 0.850/0.965,
    L2 0.646/0.951 (F1/F2). `ear-z` is a NO-OP by construction and is a wiring
    check rather than a column -- `holdout_accuracy` standardises every
    dimension on the training half itself, so handing it a pre-z-scored
    feature must reproduce `ear` exactly, and it does.

  THE DECISION STATISTIC, paired per creature -- sph minus kern, which is
  the kernel's rule with the overall MAGNITUDE removed and nothing else:
    bit F1   +0.089 +/- 0.016  (5.5 SE)   RESOLVED
    bit F2   +0.120 +/- 0.023  (5.3 SE)   RESOLVED

  SO THE ACTIONABLE RESULT IS NOT THE PRIMARY, IT IS THIS. The shipped
  index clusters a raw, ALL-POSITIVE rate vector, whose largest-variance
  direction is overall drive and carries no word. Projecting it onto the
  unit sphere before the distance is computed lifts BOTH bits, and the
  bit the lineage loses is one of them. That is one change inside the
  source-4 block, behind a genome field, and it is what to build next.

  WHAT THIS CANNOT SHOW: that a transform which helps OFFLINE helps the
  creature. These rules run on a RECORDED feature with the word order fixed;
  the kernel's prototypes move while the voice they are being used to steer
  also moves. A column that lifts the F1 bit here licenses a build, not a
  result -- exactly as partprobe's own bar was written.
